WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.775
On one Microsoft Store launch,

2
00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:06.787
I surfaced 400 craft and implementation issues in review,

3
00:00:06.787 --> 00:00:11.145
from line heights and spacing to loaders and dark-mode colors.

4
00:00:11.495 --> 00:00:14.381
They were found by eye, after the feature was built.

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.210
Every issue meant the same round trip: spot the mismatch,

6
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.834
screenshot it, write it up, file a bug, then check the fix.

7
00:00:29.184 --> 00:00:31.432
The feedback was scattered across Figma,

8
00:00:31.432 --> 00:00:32.894
Loop and the bug tracker,

9
00:00:32.894 --> 00:00:35.535
and the designer stayed the last quality gate.

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:39.829
Some craft decisions need a designer.

11
00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:42.400
Most mismatches just need measuring.

12
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.202
So I moved the check before the designer's review. Engineers drop in the design and the build,

13
00:00:50.202 --> 00:00:50.891
and run it.

14
00:00:51.241 --> 00:00:54.297
There's no setup. Two screenshots are enough to start.

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.824
Each check runs two engines. First,

16
00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:02.790
a pixel-level color diff catches exact hex mismatches.

17
00:01:03.141 --> 00:01:06.694
Then an AI vision model compares both screens for copy,

18
00:01:06.694 --> 00:01:08.596
spacing and missing elements,

19
00:01:08.596 --> 00:01:11.153
the things a pixel diff can't explain.

20
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:15.201
Every issue names the property,

21
00:01:15.201 --> 00:01:17.508
the design value, and the built value.

22
00:01:17.858 --> 00:01:18.776
Hover an issue,

23
00:01:18.776 --> 00:01:25.568
and its region lights up on the build. Filters let designers and engineers each focus on what matters to them.

24
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.826
Then it goes straight into the team's workflow.

25
00:01:30.176 --> 00:01:33.469
One click files a pre-filled bug in Azure DevOps,

26
00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:36.829
or copies every issue into Loop as a ready table.

27
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:43.817
I built Craft Check on my own with AI: a React app with Fluent UI,

28
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:44.541
a server,

29
00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:50.557
and a command line tool that also compares Figma tokens with CSS and Windows XAML.

30
00:01:50.907 --> 00:01:52.747
It's a working prototype,

31
00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:57.384
which I took to Microsoft's FHL hackathon in March 2026. Next,

32
00:01:57.384 --> 00:01:59.246
direct Figma integration,

33
00:01:59.246 --> 00:02:03.714
then running the check automatically before code is merged.

34
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.943
Automate the measuring,

35
00:02:07.943 --> 00:02:10.688
so designers can spend their time on judgment.
